12. Go-to-market strategy
I would launch through the same customer segment that provides the clearest product feedback: freelancers and small agencies.
Step 1 — Validate the problem
Interview 20–30 people who manage multiple clients or business accounts. Ask how they currently store passwords, how they share credentials, what happens when a contractor leaves, and what they dislike about existing tools.

Do not ask only whether they like your idea. Ask what they currently do and whether they would pay to solve the problem.
Step 2 — Recruit design partners
Invite 10–20 freelancers or small agency owners to test the product with non-critical accounts first. Avoid encouraging users to migrate sensitive production credentials until the product has passed the necessary security reviews.

Measure onboarding completion, time to first saved record, weekly active use, migration success, and willingness to pay.
Step 3 — Create educational content
Possible content topics include:
- Why storing client passwords in spreadsheets creates risks.
- How to organize 20 client accounts without losing track of access.
- How to share a business login without sharing access to an entire account.
- What to do when a freelancer or employee leaves.
- How to move from a browser password manager to an encrypted vault.
Publish demonstrations using fictional credentials and synthetic records. Never use genuine passwords or client information in marketing materials.
Step 4 — Use partnerships
Potential acquisition channels include:
- Web development and marketing agencies.
- Freelance communities.
- IT support providers and managed service providers.
- Cybersecurity educators and consultants.
- Small-business software communities.
Offer partner commissions only after you have sustainable subscription margins and a clear partner agreement.
Step 5 — Build trust as a marketing asset
Publish a security whitepaper, explain the encryption and recovery model in plain language, document data handling, provide a vulnerability disclosure process, and share independent assessment results when available.

Avoid unsupported claims such as “unhackable,” “military-grade,” or “100% secure.”
13. Risks and how to manage them
Risk	Mitigation
Established competitors are difficult to displace	Focus on a narrow segment and a better workflow, not feature parity everywhere
A breach could destroy trust	Minimize retained plaintext, commission external reviews, and prepare incident response
Users forget their master password	Design and test safe recovery before launch; communicate limitations clearly
Browser autofill exposes credentials to phishing	Enforce origin-aware behavior, test hostile pages, and minimize automatic exposure
Migration is too difficult	Build reliable import tools and transparent migration instructions
Customers do not want another subscription	Validate willingness to pay before expanding the product
Infrastructure and support become expensive	Limit attachment storage, establish fair usage limits, and monitor costs
Security claims exceed actual implementation	Publish only verified capabilities and maintain a documented security roadmap

The biggest risk is not a competitor launching another feature. It is building a vault that customers cannot confidently trust.
